Turnkey is one of the most-used and least-defined words in hospitality contracting. Owners
are right to ask what it actually commits a contractor to.

## It means one point of accountability, not many

A genuinely turnkey delivery means a single team is responsible for the outcome across
every discipline — design, manufacture, logistics, installation. When something needs to be
fixed on site, there is one project manager to call, not a chain of subcontractors each
pointing at the other.

## It means the space works the day the doors open

Not "the furniture has arrived." Snagged, tested, styled, and ready for a guest to check in
without anyone noticing the last coat of paint was still drying that morning.

## It means the risk sits with the contractor, not the owner

If a shipment is delayed, if a finish needs to be remade, if a subcontractor underperforms —
that is the turnkey contractor's problem to solve, on the agreed timeline, not a change
order back to the owner.

That is the standard a handover should be measured against — not whether the space looks
finished in photographs, but whether it is ready to operate.
